In [Part I of this
series](https://walker-data.com/posts/pmtiles-texas-blocks/), I showed
you how to create PMTiles from large spatial datasets and visualize them
locally with the **mapgl** and **pmtiles** R packages. We mapped all
655,000+ Census blocks in Texas with 3D extrusion, hover effects, and
tooltips - all running on your local machine.

But what if you want to share your maps with the world? Or build a
production application that others can use? In this post, I'll walk you
through deploying your PMTiles to [Cloudflare
R2](https://www.cloudflare.com/products/r2/) - a cloud storage service
that's ideal for tile hosting - and setting up [Cloudflare
Workers](https://workers.cloudflare.com/) to serve your tiles securely
and efficiently.

## Why Cloudflare R2 + Workers?

When it comes to hosting map tiles, you have several options: traditional
tile servers, cloud storage with CDN, or managed services. Cloudflare's
R2 + Workers combination offers some compelling advantages:

-   **No egress fees**: Unlike AWS S3 or Google Cloud Storage, R2
    doesn't charge for data transfer out. This is huge for map tiles,
    which can generate significant bandwidth.
-   **Global edge caching**: Workers run at Cloudflare's edge locations
    worldwide, so your tiles are served from locations close to your
    users.
-   **Access control**: With Workers, you can serve tiles via a TileJSON
    endpoint while preventing users from downloading your entire
    PMTiles file.
-   **Generous free tier**: 10GB storage and 10 million requests/month
    on the free tier covers most projects.

## Simple deployment with `pm_upload()`

The **pmtiles** package includes `pm_upload()`, which handles uploading
your PMTiles files to Cloudflare R2. Before you can use it, you'll need
to set up a few things:

### Prerequisites

1.  **Cloudflare account**: Sign up at
    [cloudflare.com](https://cloudflare.com) if you don't have one.

2.  **R2 bucket**: In your Cloudflare dashboard, go to R2 and create a
    new bucket. I'll call mine `census-blocks-tiles`.

3.  **API token**: Go to your [Cloudflare API
    Tokens](https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens) page and
    create a token with R2 read/write permissions. You'll also need your
    Account ID (found on the R2 overview page).

4.  **Environment variables**: Set these in your `.Renviron` file or
    export them in your shell:

``` bash
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=your_account_id_here
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=your_api_token_here
```

### Uploading your PMTiles

With the prerequisites in place, uploading is straightforward:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(pmtiles)

# Upload the PMTiles file we created in Part I
pm_upload(
  file = "us_blocks.pmtiles",
  bucket = "census-blocks-tiles",
  name = "us_blocks.pmtiles"
)
```
:::


The upload may take a few minutes depending on your file size and
connection speed. Our Texas blocks PMTiles file is around 150MB.

### Making the bucket public (optional)

If you just want to share your tiles publicly, you can enable public
access on your R2 bucket through the Cloudflare dashboard. This gives
you a URL like:

```
https://pub-xxxxx.r2.dev/us_blocks.pmtiles
```

You can then use this URL directly in mapgl:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(mapgl)

maplibre(
  style = openfreemap_style("positron"),
  center = c(-96.8, 32.7),
  zoom = 10,
  pitch = 45
) |>
  add_pmtiles_source(
    id = "blocks",
    url = "https://pub-xxxxx.r2.dev/us_blocks.pmtiles"
  ) |>
  add_fill_extrusion_layer(
    id = "blocks-3d",
    source = "blocks",
    source_layer = "blocks",
    fill_extrusion_color = interpolate(
      column = "population",
      values = c(0, 50, 100, 250, 1000),
      stops = c("#e3f2fd", "#90caf9", "#42a5f5", "#1e88e5", "#0d47a1")
    ),
    fill_extrusion_height = interpolate(
      column = "population",
      values = c(0, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000),
      stops = c(5, 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000)
    )
  )
```
:::


This works, but there's a catch: anyone with the URL can download your
entire PMTiles file. For public Census data, that might be fine. But if
you're building a SaaS product or working with proprietary data, you'll
want more control.

## Cloudflare Workers for secure tile serving

Cloudflare Workers let you run JavaScript at the edge to intercept and
process requests. We can use a Worker to:

1.  Serve individual tiles from the PMTiles archive
2.  Expose a TileJSON endpoint for map libraries
3.  Apply caching at the edge
4.  Prevent direct downloads of the PMTiles file

Here's how to set it up.

### Setting up the Worker project

First, install the Wrangler CLI (Cloudflare's deployment tool):

``` bash
npm install -g wrangler
wrangler login
```

Create a new Worker project:

``` bash
mkdir pmtiles-worker
cd pmtiles-worker
npm init -y
npm install pmtiles
```

### The Worker code

Create `src/index.ts` with the following code. This is adapted from
production code I'm using in my own mapping applications:


::: {.cell}

```{.typescript .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="View the complete Worker code"}
import {
  Compression,
  EtagMismatch,
  PMTiles,
  RangeResponse,
  ResolvedValueCache,
  Source,
  TileType,
} from "pmtiles";

interface Env {
  ALLOWED_ORIGINS?: string;
  BUCKET: R2Bucket;
  CACHE_CONTROL?: string;
  PUBLIC_HOSTNAME?: string;
}

// Custom error for missing archives
class KeyNotFoundError extends Error {}

// Parse tile requests: /name/z/x/y.ext or /name.json
function tile_path(path: string): {
  ok: boolean;
  name: string;
  tile: [number, number, number] | null;
  ext: string;
} {
  const p = path.replace(/^\//, "");

  // TileJSON request: /name.json
  const jsonMatch = p.match(/^([^/]+)\.json$/);
  if (jsonMatch) {
    return { ok: true, name: jsonMatch[1], tile: null, ext: "json" };
  }

  // Tile request: /name/z/x/y.ext
  const tileMatch = p.match(/^([^/]+)\/(\d+)\/(\d+)\/(\d+)\.(\w+)$/);
  if (tileMatch) {
    return {
      ok: true,
      name: tileMatch[1],
      tile: [
        parseInt(tileMatch[2]),
        parseInt(tileMatch[3]),
        parseInt(tileMatch[4]),
      ],
      ext: tileMatch[5],
    };
  }

  return { ok: false, name: "", tile: null, ext: "" };
}

// Get PMTiles path in bucket
function pmtiles_path(name: string): string {
  return `${name}.pmtiles`;
}

// Handle gzip decompression
async function nativeDecompress(
  buf: ArrayBuffer,
  compression: Compression
): Promise<ArrayBuffer> {
  if (compression === Compression.None || compression === Compression.Unknown) {
    return buf;
  }
  if (compression === Compression.Gzip) {
    const stream = new Response(buf).body;
    const result = stream?.pipeThrough(new DecompressionStream("gzip"));
    return new Response(result).arrayBuffer();
  }
  throw new Error("Compression method not supported");
}

const CACHE = new ResolvedValueCache(25, undefined, nativeDecompress);

// R2 data source for PMTiles
class R2Source implements Source {
  env: Env;
  archiveName: string;

  constructor(env: Env, archiveName: string) {
    this.env = env;
    this.archiveName = archiveName;
  }

  getKey() {
    return this.archiveName;
  }

  async getBytes(
    offset: number,
    length: number,
    signal?: AbortSignal,
    etag?: string
  ): Promise<RangeResponse> {
    const resp = await this.env.BUCKET.get(pmtiles_path(this.archiveName), {
      range: { offset: offset, length: length },
      onlyIf: { etagMatches: etag },
    });

    if (!resp) {
      throw new KeyNotFoundError("Archive not found");
    }

    const o = resp as R2ObjectBody;
    if (!o.body) {
      throw new EtagMismatch();
    }

    const a = await o.arrayBuffer();
    return {
      data: a,
      etag: o.etag,
      cacheControl: o.httpMetadata?.cacheControl,
      expires: o.httpMetadata?.cacheExpiry?.toISOString(),
    };
  }
}

export default {
  async fetch(request: Request, env: Env, ctx: ExecutionContext): Promise<Response> {
    if (request.method.toUpperCase() === "POST") {
      return new Response(undefined, { status: 405 });
    }

    const url = new URL(request.url);
    const { ok, name, tile, ext } = tile_path(url.pathname);

    const cache = caches.default;

    if (!ok) {
      return new Response("Invalid URL", { status: 404 });
    }

    // Handle CORS
    let allowedOrigin = "";
    if (typeof env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS !== "undefined") {
      for (const o of env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS.split(",")) {
        if (o === request.headers.get("Origin") || o === "*") {
          allowedOrigin = o;
        }
      }
    }

    // Check cache first
    const cached = await cache.match(request.url);
    if (cached) {
      const respHeaders = new Headers(cached.headers);
      if (allowedOrigin) {
        respHeaders.set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", allowedOrigin);
      }
      respHeaders.set("Vary", "Origin");
      return new Response(cached.body, {
        headers: respHeaders,
        status: cached.status,
      });
    }

    // Helper to create cacheable responses
    const cacheableResponse = (
      body: ArrayBuffer | string | undefined,
      cacheableHeaders: Headers,
      status: number
    ) => {
      cacheableHeaders.set(
        "Cache-Control",
        env.CACHE_CONTROL || "public, max-age=86400"
      );

      const cacheable = new Response(body, {
        headers: cacheableHeaders,
        status: status,
      });

      ctx.waitUntil(cache.put(request.url, cacheable));

      const respHeaders = new Headers(cacheableHeaders);
      if (allowedOrigin) {
        respHeaders.set("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", allowedOrigin);
      }
      respHeaders.set("Vary", "Origin");
      return new Response(body, { headers: respHeaders, status: status });
    };

    const cacheableHeaders = new Headers();
    const source = new R2Source(env, name);
    const p = new PMTiles(source, CACHE, nativeDecompress);

    try {
      const pHeader = await p.getHeader();

      // Handle TileJSON request
      if (!tile) {
        cacheableHeaders.set("Content-Type", "application/json");
        const t = await p.getTileJson(
          `https://${env.PUBLIC_HOSTNAME || url.hostname}/${name}`
        );
        return cacheableResponse(JSON.stringify(t), cacheableHeaders, 200);
      }

      // Validate zoom level
      if (tile[0] < pHeader.minZoom || tile[0] > pHeader.maxZoom) {
        return cacheableResponse(undefined, cacheableHeaders, 404);
      }

      // Validate tile type matches request
      for (const pair of [
        [TileType.Mvt, "mvt"],
        [TileType.Png, "png"],
        [TileType.Jpeg, "jpg"],
        [TileType.Webp, "webp"],
        [TileType.Avif, "avif"],
      ]) {
        if (pHeader.tileType === pair[0] && ext !== pair[1]) {
          if (pHeader.tileType === TileType.Mvt && ext === "pbf") {
            continue;
          }
          return cacheableResponse(
            `Bad request: requested .${ext} but archive has type .${pair[1]}`,
            cacheableHeaders,
            400
          );
        }
      }

      // Get the tile
      const tiledata = await p.getZxy(tile[0], tile[1], tile[2]);

      // Set content type based on tile type
      switch (pHeader.tileType) {
        case TileType.Mvt:
          cacheableHeaders.set("Content-Type", "application/x-protobuf");
          break;
        case TileType.Png:
          cacheableHeaders.set("Content-Type", "image/png");
          break;
        case TileType.Jpeg:
          cacheableHeaders.set("Content-Type", "image/jpeg");
          break;
        case TileType.Webp:
          cacheableHeaders.set("Content-Type", "image/webp");
          break;
      }

      if (tiledata) {
        return cacheableResponse(tiledata.data, cacheableHeaders, 200);
      }
      return cacheableResponse(undefined, cacheableHeaders, 204);
    } catch (e) {
      if (e instanceof KeyNotFoundError) {
        return cacheableResponse("Archive not found", cacheableHeaders, 404);
      }
      throw e;
    }
  },
};
```
:::


### Configuration with wrangler.toml

Create a `wrangler.toml` file to configure your Worker:

```toml
name = "census-blocks-tiles"
main = "src/index.ts"
compatibility_date = "2024-01-01"

[vars]
ALLOWED_ORIGINS = "*"
CACHE_CONTROL = "public, max-age=86400"

[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "BUCKET"
bucket_name = "census-blocks-tiles"
```

Key configuration options:

-   `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`: Set to `"*"` for public access, or specify your
    domain(s) like `"https://myapp.com,https://localhost:3838"`.
-   `CACHE_CONTROL`: How long tiles are cached at the edge (86400 =
    24 hours).
-   `bucket_name`: Must match your R2 bucket name.

### Deploying the Worker

Deploy with a single command:

``` bash
wrangler deploy
```

Your Worker will be available at a URL like:

```
https://census-blocks-tiles.your-subdomain.workers.dev
```

## Using your deployed tiles in mapgl

Now you can use your Worker's TileJSON endpoint in mapgl. The URL
pattern is:

-   TileJSON: `https://your-worker.workers.dev/us_blocks.json`
-   Individual tiles:
    `https://your-worker.workers.dev/us_blocks/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`

Here's how to use it:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(mapgl)

maplibre(
  style = openfreemap_style("positron"),
  center = c(-96.8, 32.7),
  zoom = 10,
  pitch = 45,
  hash = TRUE
) |>
  add_vector_source(
    id = "blocks",
    url = "https://census-blocks-tiles.your-subdomain.workers.dev/us_blocks.json"
  ) |>
  add_fill_extrusion_layer(
    id = "blocks-3d",
    source = "blocks",
    source_layer = "blocks",
    fill_extrusion_color = interpolate(
      column = "population",
      values = c(0, 50, 100, 250, 1000),
      stops = c("#e3f2fd", "#90caf9", "#42a5f5", "#1e88e5", "#0d47a1")
    ),
    fill_extrusion_height = interpolate(
      column = "population",
      values = c(0, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1000),
      stops = c(5, 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000)
    ),
    fill_extrusion_opacity = 0.85,
    hover_options = list(
      fill_extrusion_color = "#ffd700"
    ),
    tooltip = concat(
      "Block: ", get_column("geoid"),
      "<br>Population: ", get_column("population"),
      "<br>Housing units: ", get_column("housing")
    )
  )
```
:::


Notice we're using `add_vector_source()` with the TileJSON URL instead
of `add_pmtiles_source()`. The Worker handles all the PMTiles magic
behind the scenes.

## Cost considerations

One of the best things about this setup is the cost:

| Service | Free Tier | Paid Pricing |
|---------|-----------|--------------|
| R2 Storage | 10 GB/month | $0.015/GB/month |
| R2 Operations | 10M Class B ops | $0.36 per million |
| Workers Requests | 100K/day | $0.50 per million |
| Workers CPU | 10ms/request | Included |

For comparison, a traditional tile server on a VPS might cost $20-50/month
before you even consider bandwidth. And services like Mapbox charge based
on map loads, which can add up quickly for popular applications.

With R2 + Workers:

-   **No egress fees** means you don't pay per tile served
-   **Edge caching** means popular tiles are served from cache, reducing
    R2 operations
-   Most projects will stay well within the free tier

## Next steps

We now have our Census block tiles deployed to the cloud and accessible
via a secure, cached endpoint. In Part III, I'll show you how to build a
production Shiny application around these tiles and deploy it as a
SaaS - complete with user authentication and payment processing.

The full Worker code from this post is available in the [blog post
companion
repository](https://github.com/walkerke/walkerke.github.io/tree/master/posts/pmtiles-cloudflare-r2).
If you want to learn more about building modern mapping applications
with R, check out my [mapgl workshop
series](https://walkerdata.gumroad.com/l/modern-mapping-bundle).
